export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","addon":"nesting","name":"Nesting","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"nesting-start","scope":"addon:nesting","title":"Nest outlines on plates or a roll","summary":"Lay the closed outlines of your drawing on plates or a roll with as little waste as it can, draw the sheets beside the drawing and save them as DXF for the cutter.","section":"tasks","order":1,"keywords":["nest","nesting","sheet","plate","roll","cut","laser","plasma","router","waterjet","cnc","waste","efficiency","dxf"],"headings":[{"id":"nest-the-outlines","text":"Nest the outlines","level":2},{"id":"save-it-for-the-cutter","text":"Save it for the cutter","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"nest-the-outlines\"\u003ENest the outlines\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESwitch the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons. Select the closed outlines to cut — closed polylines (their arcs stay arcs) and circles; a closed shape inside another is a hole of it — and type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003ENEST\u003C/code\u003E (or \u003Ccode\u003ENST\u003C/code\u003E). Answer:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPlates\u003C/strong\u003E (length and width) or a \u003Cstrong\u003ERoll\u003C/strong\u003E (its width);\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ethe \u003Cstrong\u003Emargin\u003C/strong\u003E kept clear at the edges and the \u003Cstrong\u003Espace\u003C/strong\u003E between parts;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ehow many \u003Cstrong\u003Ecopies\u003C/strong\u003E of each part;\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003Ewhether parts may \u003Cstrong\u003Eturn\u003C/strong\u003E: by 90 degrees, by any angle (15° steps) or not at all.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe sheets are drawn to the right of the drawing on layer NEST-SHEET, with the parts on NEST-PARTS and their names on NEST-LABELS. Your own outlines are not moved. The command line says how many plates (or how much roll) it used, the share of material the parts take, and anything that did not fit. The layout is checked again on the exact shapes, so no two parts overlap or come closer than the spacing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"save-it-for-the-cutter\"\u003ESave it for the cutter\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFile › Export › \u003Cstrong\u003ENested sheets (DXF)\u003C/strong\u003E (or \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E on the command line) writes the last nest as a DXF (layers SHEET, PARTS, LABELS). Lead-ins, the cutting order and common-line cutting are your cutter&#039;s software&#039;s job.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe nesting is the same engine as PartCraft&#039;s Export › Nest, which also unfolds sheet metal parts.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Nest the outlines Switch the add-on on in Settings › Add-ons. Select the closed outlines to cut — closed polylines (their arcs stay arcs) and circles; a closed shape inside another is a hole of it — and type NEST (or NST). Answer: Plates (length and width) or a Roll (its width); the margin kept clear at the edges and the space between parts; how many copies of each part; whether parts may turn: by 90 degrees, by any angle (15° steps) or not at all. The sheets are drawn to the right of the drawing on layer NEST-SHEET, with the parts on NEST-PARTS and their names on NEST-LABELS. Your own outlines are not moved. The command line says how many plates (or how much roll) it used, the share of material the parts take, and anything that did not fit. The layout is checked again on the exact shapes, so no two parts overlap or come closer than the spacing. Save it for the cutter File › Export › Nested sheets (DXF) (or EXPORT on the command line) writes the last nest as a DXF (layers SHEET, PARTS, LABELS). Lead-ins, the cutting order and common-line cutting are your cutter's software's job. The nesting is the same engine as PartCraft's Export › Nest, which also unfolds sheet metal parts."}]};
